Date: Thu, 18 Feb 93 13:40:42 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: LAW: "initiating force"

I've seen it said that libertarians oppose "initiating force".  But
how is that defined?

As far as I can tell, it's LC to use force to keep someone from
trespassing on my land, but it's not LC to use force to keep someone
from walking on their land.  The point that bothers me is that both
are physically the same action; it's the "social context" that makes
the difference.  This leads me to believe that the phrase "initiating
force" must incorporate (implicity) all those social context effects,
which may wind up just incorporating all of our preconceived notions
of right and wrong.  This could become a real problem, because then
the standard of "initiating force" isn't really independent of our
concepts of right, law, justice, etc.

What's the exit from this circularity?

Dale

