Date: Thu, 18 Feb 93 14:36:07 -0500
From: andreag@csmil.umich.edu
Subject: BRAIN: uploading 

David Cake writes:

>	I remain an uploading sceptic (at least for any time soon). However
>the similar but different scenario of gradually integrating more and more
>computer systems within the living brain ( until eventually they are a more
>important component) is a bit more feasible.

Your objections to not-fully-brute-force methods of uploading are valid,
but don't apply to a truly brute-force methods.  They apply in spades to 
your alternative of neural implants.

If we don't understand the interconnectivity in the brain, swapping in new
versions of old systems (chips for certain parts of the brain) requires
either a ton of empirical attemtps (basically a random search of a large
solution space) or a fundamental understanding of EXACTLY how different
systems interconnect.  Just what we won't have for a good while.

>PS The problem with brute force uploading (simulating each individual neuron)
>is that we don't have anything like the scanning tech needed to completely 
>a neuron without removing it from the living brain. It is much more complicated
>than you might think (don't be fooled by overly simplistic neural network
> models).

I'll grant you that brute force uploading is harder than people seem to
think it is.  Still, I suspect it is not harder than the complete understanding
of the brain.  I'll bet (Robin?  do we have our futures market yet?) that
the scanning technology appears before we can build a model of an individual
from theory and scientific understanding.

p.s.  simplistic neural networks are more an example of trying to model
the brain with abstractions than of replicating it.  The hope is that
brute force uploading would avoid the problems of neural nets.

Andrea


