Date: Thu, 18 Feb 93 15:52:33 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Predicting the Stock Market

Timothy C. May writes:
>By the way, many of these topics, related to stock markets, gambling,
>complexity theory, compressibility, inference, hypothesis testing, etc.,
>are covered in a wonderful book called "Elements of Information Theory," by
>Cover and Thomas, 1991. There's even a section on how Kolmogorov-Chaitin
>algorithmic complexity explains why Occam's Razor works! There's also an
>information-theoretic treatment of asset portfolio pricing, with some of
>the same arguments about asset allocation as a function of risk that Hal
>Finney referred to earlier tonight. (But I haven't read the section
>yet...arguing about zero-sum games seems to be a zero-sum game these last
>few weeks.)

This is a good book in general.  Since this book, Cover has done
interesting work in analyzing ideal investment portfolios.  In analogy
with a "universal" probability distribution, which contains finite
weights on all finitely describle hypotheses, a universal portolio
contains a finite fraction of every possible (finite) investment
strategy.  Asymptotically, this portfolio will have the same
exponential growth rate as the very best possible strategy as judged
with hindsight!  This make no assumptions like CAPM does about the
distribution of returns.  FYI.

Robin 

