than t1 ) in the case of function g.

Assumptions:
------------

  1.  The parameters involved ( road conditions, weather, etc ), are
      the same for functions f and g.  The only different variable is
      speed, which is greater for f(t) than for g(t).

  2.  The value of f(t) at any point t is slightly greater than g(t),
      since we assume increasing speed parameters increase the percentage
      of having a wreck.

  3.  We arrive at our destination in time t1 for function f and time
      t2 ( > t1 ) for function g.

My conjecture/theory:
---------------------
  1.  My conjecture is that the increased chance of a wreck due to
      increased speed ( represented by the area under the f(t) curve
      minus the area under the g(t) curve, labelled "A1" on the graph
      above ) is less than the increased chance of a wreck due to
      having to drive a longer time ( represented by the area under
      the curve g(t2 - t1), labelled "A2" on the graph above.

      In short, it all boils down to whether A1 > A2 ( "speed kills"
      scenario ) or A2 > A1 ( "speed doesn't necessarilt kill, and
      may in fact decrease the chance of a wreck, especially on a longer
      trip" ).

Other possible conclusions:
---------------------------

  1.  The extra fatigue factor travelling at a slower speed ( especially
      near the end of a long trip ) may be a greater contributor to
      wreck probabilities than the increasing chance of a wreck from
      increased speed.  This is evidenced in France every year as the
      national Police predict with great accuracy where vacationing
      Parisioners will wreck their vehicles on their way to the beaches
      in southern France.  They just calculate how far people drive after
      6, 8, or 10 hours at various speeds to see where they'll fall asleep,
      and then send alert the wreckers and ambulances about these spots
      ahead of time.  This information is from my high-school French
      teacher who lived in France for several years.

Other considerations:
---------------------
  1.  This assumes that the results of a wreck are equally bad for
      speeds done in f(t) and g(t).  In other words, a wreck at 55 MPH
      is just as bad as a wreck at 70 MPH.  This would have to be
      researched further.  I suspect there is a hyperbolic fatality
      function that approaches the vertical asymptote for speeds in
      excess of 45 MPH, even with the introduction of air-bags.


My speculations:
----------------
    I haven't had time to research this further, to see if there
are actual studies to indicate which scenario is true, yet I
tend to believe that "speed doesn't necessarily kill".

    I truly doubt, however, that any lawmakers anywhere in Alabama
( or the U.S. ) would understand any of these risk/benefit
assessment functions, much less change any existing speed limit
laws based on scientific studies confirming one theory or another.

    Basically, nowadays, the State just uses traffic laws as a
means of revenue generation, to shake down the citizenry
for lotsa bucks.  I've got the figures for the city of Madison
at home, and they were incredible for 1990.

    Ponder this:  When the US Government lowered the speed limit
from 65 to 55 MPH, was it because the roads were suddenly unsafe
at 65 ?  And, when they later raised it back to 65, was it because
the roads had suddenly become safe at 65 again overnight ?  Or,
is it because the federal speed limit is an arbitrary number
which is changed at the whim of lawmakers ?  If so, why is an
arbitrary number picked by folks in Washington better than a
number I pick based on current driving conditions while I'm on
the interstate ?

    Also, what is it about stretches of interstates passing through
towns of population > XX that makes them inherently more unsafe, so
that the speed limit must be lowered to 55 MPH in these areas ?
Is it because they have more traffic ?  Not in most of the cases
I've noticed.  Is it because they have on/off ramps ?  Then why
not have all on/off ramp areas on interstates be 55 MPH ?  Or, is
it because local townships want to squeeze cash out of folks
who don't reset their cruise controls when driving through these areas ?

    Also, on a related topic, why is it not okay to slow down to
1 MPH at a stop sign, instead of absolute 0 MPH ?

    Also, don't you think it's more dangerous for flammable-material
carrying trucks to have to stop at all RR tracks ( even ones with
gates and lights, like the one at Hwy 20 and Intergraph Way ),
than to just drive on through them if a train isn't coming ?

    If I'm supposedly responsible enough to vote ( not for Clinton )
and drive a car, why am I not allowed to determine the speed
at which I drive on an interstate ?

    Also, have you ever been driving down an interstate, and suddenly
come upon a cluster of about 30 vehicles in both lanes doing
55 or 65 MPH, because a policeman is at the head of the pack ?
Don't try to tell me that this "legal" situation is safer than if
the policeman weren't around and the drivers had naturally spread
their vehicles along the road at their own pace, instead of being
stuck in a moving traffic jam with cars on all sides.

    Why do German autobahns have no speed limits, but American
interstates do ?  Do Germans have more hand-eye coordination
than Americans ?  Do they have better-made cars ?  Are they
inherently a "master race" ?

